          "deepDive": "**〜たて** attaches to the ます-stem and means *just ~ed, freshly ~ed*. It usually works as a noun, so it is followed by の + noun (炊きたてのご飯) or だ／です (ペンキぬりたてだ), or takes particles directly (できたてを買ってきた).\n\nWhere you meet it:\n- **Food**: 焼きたて, 炊きたて, 揚げたて, 搾りたて, とれたて (freshly harvested), いれたて (freshly brewed), できたて, 作りたて.\n- **New status / knowledge**: 社会人になりたて, 覚えたての歌 — the person is still new at it.\n- **Signs**: ペンキ塗りたて *wet paint*.\n\nThe feeling is usually positive (freshness), but with people it can hint at inexperience: 入社したての新人 *a brand-new recruit*.\n\nContrast with neighbors:\n- **〜かけ** = *half-done, started but not finished*: 書きかけのレポート, 飲みかけのジュース. たて is *just finished*; かけ is *not finished yet*. This pair is exactly what the practice tests.\n- **V-たばかり** (N4) can follow almost any verb and is a full clause: 昨日買ったばかりの傘. たて is limited to a small set of verbs: ✗行きたて sounds wrong, and 買いたて is heard only colloquially (買ったばかり is the standard way).\n\nCommon mistake: attaching たて to the dictionary form (✗焼くたて) or to the た-form (✗焼いたて). Always use the stem: 焼き＋たて.",
