          "deepDive": "**AどころかB** literally means *it's no place for A* — i.e. *far from A / let alone A*. It has two directions, and telling them apart is the key:\n\n- **Opposite** (*far from A, actually B*): 暑いどころかすごく寒かった; やせるどころか体重が増えた. B contradicts the expectation A.\n- **Further in the same direction** (*let alone A, not even B*): 海外旅行どころか国内旅行もしたことがない; 漢字どころかひらがなも読めない. B is a more extreme case, often marked by **も／さえ／まで**.\n\nA is typically a word the other person just used (A：静かでしょう？ B：静かどころか…) or the speaker's first assumption, which is why it's so common in conversation.\n\nConnection: plain form; な-adjectives and nouns attach directly without だ (静かどころか, 海外旅行どころか).\n\nContrast (see the ～ところ／どころ list, p.223):\n- **〜どころじゃない／どころではない** (#38) = *this is no time for ~ / I can't possibly ~*: 忙しくて旅行どころじゃない. It ends the thought; どころか links to a second clause.\n- **〜ところだった** (#89) = *almost ~* — only looks similar.\n- **〜ばかりか** (*not only… but also*) only goes in the “further” direction and has no “opposite” reading.\n\nCommon pitfall in exercises: after 〜どころか, B must be **more extreme**, not less. 5キロどころか1キロも走れなかった ✓ (1 km is easier than 5, and even that was impossible); ✗5キロどころか10キロも走れなかった doesn't escalate logically. Likewise 車を買うどころか自転車を買うのも無理だ.",
